<section id="s1">
<h2>1 Executive Summary</h2>
<p>The calculations module is a collection of engineering and lifecycle-cost functions. Analysis is performed to determine how its documented interfaces support a public cost-discounting example, and the key results are summarized below.</p>
<ul>
<li>The public replacement and maintenance examples are calculated separately and combined as an illustration.
<ul><li>The combined present cost is {{ n('combined_pv') }} USD at the source example rate of {{ n('rate_percent') }} % over {{ n('years') }} years. Its equivalent annual cost is {{ n('annual_cost') }} USD/year. The calculation basis is given in <a href="#s4">Section 4</a>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Cost timing can be checked using the reported inputs and the cumulative-cost chart.
<ul><li>Budget acceptance is not evaluated because no budget or competing design is specified. Structural qualification is not established by this example; the additional evidence is identified in <a href="#s8">Section 8</a>.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
<h3>Revision history</h3>
<p>The revision record is presented below.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Revision</th><th>Date</th><th>Change and reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>22 October 2026</td><td>First module description with public cost examples, explicit timing and bounded validation evidence.</td></tr></tbody><caption>Revision history. The initial issue establishes the calculation basis.</caption></table></div>
</section>
<section id="s2">
<h2>2 Introduction</h2>
<p>The implemented calculation areas and the selected workflow are described in this section. Direct function calls are provided for scalar and list calculations; a complete design model is not assembled by the module.</p>
<p>The lifecycle-cost workflow is covered in full from timed cash inputs through present cost to annual equivalent cost. It is selected because its documented interfaces, numerical tests and public reference examples permit a readable arithmetic check without material coefficients or licensed design tables.</p>
<h3>2.1 Not covered</h3>
<p>The remaining areas are identified without extending the worked-example conclusion to their results.</p>
<p>Riser vibration, array interference, riser dynamics, drilling-riser tension and integrity, wellhead fatigue, steel catenary riser fatigue, tension-leg platform geometry, pipeline resistance and casing ratings are not evaluated here. The polynomial evaluator is a placeholder that returns no numerical value. The source and test inventory is provided in <a href="#s9-2">Section 9.2</a>.</p>
<p>Within lifecycle costing, levelized output cost, event-based maintenance, transport, downtime and installed-cost summation are additional callable utilities. Their input contracts are listed in <a href="#s10-contract">Appendix 10.2</a>; their use in an asset forecast is outside the selected discounting example.</p>
</section>
<section id="s3">
<h2>3 Summary of Results</h2>
<p>The time-dependent present cost of the combined illustration is presented below. Each point is calculated from the costs incurred up to that year, discounted to the same base date.</p>
<p>The combined present cost is {{ n('combined_pv') }} USD at the source example rate of {{ n('rate_percent') }} % over {{ n('years') }} years. Its equivalent annual cost is {{ n('annual_cost') }} USD/year. The replacement payment occurs at the final year end; maintenance occurs at each year end.</p>
