<section id="s4">
<h2>4 Basis and Criteria</h2>
<p>The public source examples and numerical comparison criteria are defined here. NIST Handbook 135, August 2025 edition, is used for discounting theory and example inputs, as identified in <a href="#ref-nist">Reference 1</a>.</p>
<p>The replacement example uses {{ n('replacement') }} USD at the end of year {{ n('years') }}. The maintenance example uses {{ n('maintenance') }} USD at each year end over the same period. Both use an annual discount rate of {{ n('rate_percent') }} %. This rate is an example input, not a statement of the current rate required for an investment decision.</p>
<p>Each published example is compared with the module result to the displayed cent. The report tests additionally use a tighter arithmetic tolerance for the hand-worked anchors. This is a reporting-accuracy criterion, without a structural resistance criterion or an investment-acceptance threshold.</p>
<p>No material strength, load factor, design fatigue factor or environmental limit is selected. These quantities remain required inputs for a future structural assessment. No numerical design requirement is taken from a licensed publication.</p>
</section>
<section id="s5">
<h2>5 Methodology</h2>
<p>The present cost is calculated by discounting each dated payment and summing the resulting base-date values. The equivalent annual cost is calculated from that present cost using the same annual rate and study period.</p>
<h3>5.1 Governing equations</h3>
<p>The single-payment relation in <a href="#ref-nist">Reference 1</a>, section 3.2.1, equation (3−6), is applied to each payment.</p>
<p class="formula">P = Σ C<sub>t</sub> / (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></p>
<p>For equal end-of-year maintenance payments, the finite geometric sum is the uniform-present-value relation in section 3.2.2.1, equation (3−7).</p>
<p class="formula">P<sub>maintenance</sub> = M [(1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1] / [r (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>]</p>
<p>The annual-value relation in section 17.4, equation (12−17), is applied to the combined present cost.</p>
<p class="formula">A = P r / [1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>]</p>
<p>The symbols are defined in <a href="#s10-symbols">Appendix 10.1</a>. The unit terms in the equations follow the cited mathematical definitions; no empirical coefficient is introduced.</p>
<h3>5.2 Timing boundary</h3>
<p>The example cash flow is represented on an annual timeline. This is an economic model, so no structural geometry, support conditions or mesh is implied.</p>
<figure>
<svg class="timeline" viewBox="0 0 720 195" role="img" aria-label="Cash-flow boundary: annual maintenance at year ends and replacement at the final year end">
<rect x="8" y="8" width="704" height="175" rx="10"/>
