</section>
<section id="s6">
<h2>6 Worked Example</h2>
<p>The published replacement and maintenance cases are calculated separately before their cash flows are combined. The combined case is an illustration constructed from those two examples; it is not presented as a third published benchmark.</p>
<p>The input replacement is {{ n('replacement') }} USD at the end of year {{ n('years') }}. The input maintenance is {{ n('maintenance') }} USD at each year end. The annual discount rate is {{ n('rate_percent') }} %, and the duration is {{ n('years') }} years. The source is <a href="#ref-nist">the NIST handbook reference</a>.</p>
<p>The replacement present cost returned by the module is {{ n('replacement_pv') }} USD. The maintenance present cost returned by the module is {{ n('maintenance_pv') }} USD. Each agrees with its published example to the displayed cent.</p>
<p>The combined present cost returned by the module is {{ n('combined_pv') }} USD. The equivalent annual cost returned by the module is {{ n('annual_cost') }} USD/year. The replacement contributes {{ n('replacement_annual') }} USD/year and maintenance contributes {{ n('maintenance_annual') }} USD/year to that annual equivalent.</p>
<p>No initial purchase, revenue, tax, escalation or residual value is included. An asset-level economic decision is not established by this limited cash-flow illustration.</p>
</section>
<section id="s7">
<h2>7 Validation Evidence</h2>
<p>The existing repository tests and the additional report checks are distinguished here. The inspected calculation test files contain {{ existing_tests }} test functions; this inventory describes the evidence available, without converting the count into a physical-validation claim.</p>
<p>The existing cost tests exercise a timed cost stream, levelized output cost, equivalent annual cost, maintenance cost, transport cost, downtime adjustment and installed-cost addition. Empty cash streams and selected invalid denominators are also covered. The cost-test assertions and the source inventory are located in <a href="#s9-2">Section 9.2</a>.</p>
<p>The report tests add hand-worked anchors for replacement present cost, maintenance present cost and combined annual cost. Consistency checks compare the displayed values and chart data with fresh module calls. A rebuild comparison checks the committed document, its change panel and calculation-source digests.</p>
<p>The public NIST examples establish arithmetic agreement for the stated timing and inputs. Field validation, a complete asset cost estimate and qualification of the structural routines are not established by those checks. The inspected cost tests contain illustrative values rather than a measured cost dataset or a whole-asset published benchmark.</p>
</section>
<section id="s8">
<h2>8 Limits and Recommendations</h2>
<p>The limits affecting interpretation and the required next evidence are stated below. Budget acceptance is not evaluated, and structural qualification is not established by the selected cost example.</p>
<ul>
<li>Restrict this example to deterministic, annually dated cost arithmetic.
<ul><li>Supply a complete cost register, a stated currency and price date, consistent escalation and discounting, and comparable service before selecting an asset alternative. This follows the study-basis and discounting treatment in <a href="#ref-nist">Reference 1</a>, chapters 2 and 3.</li></ul></li>
<li>Check input domains before using the cost utilities.
<ul><li>The annual-cost function divides by zero for a zero discount rate or zero duration. Levelized cost divides by zero for zero discounted output and does not reject unequal stream lengths. Event-based maintenance divides by the supplied mean time between failures. Add explicit domain handling and tests before exposing these inputs to an unattended decision tool.</li></ul></li>
<li>Resolve the structural calculation basis before design use.
<ul><li>The natural-frequency, cross-flow response and buckle-arrest expressions contain dimensional inconsistencies with their stated output units. Casing collapse includes approximate regime selection. These routines require dimensional correction, edition-specific source checking and reference cases before their outputs are used as design evidence. Source locations are given in <a href="#s9-2">Section 9.2</a>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Complete the public interface where numerical behavior is absent.
<ul><li>The polynomial evaluator requires a defined coefficient order, units, input domain and value assertions before a polynomial result is reported.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="s9">
<h2>9 References</h2>
<p>The public theory source and the inspected repository evidence are identified here. Numerical design provisions from unread or licensed publications are not reproduced.</p>
<h3>9.1 Public source</h3>
<p id="ref-nist">Reference 1. <a href="{{ nist_url }}">NIST HB 135e2025, Life Cycle Costing Manual for the Federal Energy Management Program</a>, August 2025 edition. Sections 3.2.1 and 3.2.2.1 provide the replacement and maintenance examples; section 17.4 provides annual value. Chapters 2 and 3 define the study basis and discounting assumptions. The public publication was accessed on 22 October 2026.</p>
<h3 id="s9-2">9.2 Repository evidence</h3>
<p>The calculation sources and existing test inventory are listed below. The cost entry points are <code>net_present_value(costs, discount_rate)</code> and <code>annual_equivalent_cost(npv, discount_rate, life_years)</code>, both documented in the cost source.</p>
<p>The existing cost evidence is <code>tests/modules/calculations/test_lifecycle_cost.py</code>. The report checks are <code>tests/modules/calculations/test_module_report.py</code>. The module overview is <code>src/assetutilities/calculations/docs/agent-pack.md</code>. Its documented dimensional concerns are checked against the inspected function bodies rather than used as numerical input.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Calculation source</th><th>Existing test functions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for source in data.sources %}<tr><td><code>{{ source.path }}</code></td><td>{{ source.tests }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody><caption>Table 1. Existing test-function inventory by calculation source. Corresponding test files use the same module name with a test prefix.</caption></table></div>
<p>The dimensional limitations concern <code>riser_natural_frequency</code> in <code>riser_viv.py</code>, <code>crossflow_psd_amplitude</code> in <code>riser_dynamics.py</code>, and <code>buckle_arrest_capacity</code> in <code>pipeline_dnv.py</code>. With the documented inputs, their expressions have dimensions of length per time, length per time and force, respectively, rather than the stated frequency, displacement and pressure.</p>
<p>The calculation is repeated using the documented public entry points as follows. Cost lists begin at the base date; the first nonzero payment is therefore preceded by a zero.</p>
<pre class="formula"><code>from assetutilities.calculations.lifecycle_cost import (
    net_present_value, annual_equivalent_cost,
)
replacement = [0] * {{ number('years') }} + [{{ number('replacement')|replace(',', '') }}]
maintenance = [0] + [{{ number('maintenance') }}] * {{ number('years') }}
costs = [a + b for a, b in zip(replacement, maintenance)]
rate = {{ data['values'].rate_percent / 100 }}
present_cost = net_present_value(costs, rate)
annual_cost = annual_equivalent_cost(present_cost, rate, {{ number('years') }})</code></pre>
<p>The exact rebuild command is <code>PYTHONPATH=src python scripts/reports/build_calculations_report.py</code>.</p>
<p>The inlined shared presentation source is <code>digitalmodel:src/digitalmodel/reporting/assets/calc_report_format.html</code>; its stylesheet is preserved without changes. No report using the requested body class existed in this repository at the inspected base.</p>
</section>
<section id="s10">
<h2>10 Appendices</h2>
<p>The symbols, input contracts, cash-flow details and missing-data register are provided for calculation checking. The quality checks are stated with their evidence and limits.</p>
<h3 id="s10-symbols">10.1 Acronyms and symbols</h3>
<p>The notation used in the equations is defined here.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Definition</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>P</td><td>Present cost at the base date</td><td>USD</td></tr><tr><td>C<sub>t</sub></td><td>Payment at time t</td><td>USD</td></tr><tr><td>M</td><td>Equal annual maintenance payment</td><td>USD/year</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Equivalent annual cost</td><td>USD/year</td></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>Annual discount rate as a decimal</td><td>Dimensionless</td></tr><tr><td>t, n</td><td>Elapsed years and total study period</td><td>year</td></tr><tr><td>USD</td><td>United States dollar, as used in the federal handbook example</td><td>Currency</td></tr><tr><td>NIST</td><td>National Institute of Standards and Technology</td><td>Not applicable</td></tr></tbody><caption>Table 2. Symbols and abbreviations used in this report.</caption></table></div>
<h3 id="s10-contract">10.2 Input and output contracts</h3>
<p>The selected functions consume caller-defined cash streams and return scalar values. No unit conversion, price-date reconciliation or source lookup is performed by those functions.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Inputs</th><th>Output and domain concern</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Present cost</td><td>Annual cost list, base-date element first; decimal annual rate</td><td>Caller currency. An empty list returns zero; the caller establishes valid finite rates and costs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annual equivalent cost</td><td>Present cost, decimal annual rate, integer duration in years</td><td>Currency/year. Zero rate and zero duration are not handled.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Levelized output cost</td><td>Costs, outputs and annual rate</td><td>Currency/output unit. Equal timing and nonzero discounted output are caller obligations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Event-based maintenance</td><td>Cost per event, mean time between failures in years, elapsed years</td><td>Currency. Positive mean time between failures and an appropriate event-rate model are required.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transport and installed cost</td><td>Cost components, day rate and days where applicable</td><td>Currency. Matching currency and price date are required.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effective transit duration</td><td>Nominal days and downtime fraction</td><td>Days. A fraction below zero or at least unity is rejected; a negative nominal duration is not rejected.</td></tr>
</tbody><caption>Table 3. Lifecycle-cost input contracts and enforced or caller-supplied restrictions.</caption></table></div>
